  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the locker room, Fedya checks on Senya who is feeling very sick. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не ешь эту рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты уголь пил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ага, две пачки сожрал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ни хрена себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня болит живот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе надо пойти домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу работать сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Иди домой и отдыхай.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you all right?' Fedya puts 'you' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I am very bad.' Notice the word order: 'to me' comes first, and 'badly' finishes the sentence. Fedya answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I told you.' Here 'I' sets up the idea, while 'said' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не ешь эту рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not eat that fish.' You'll hear 'eat' all the time in the show, here connected with 'fish'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I thought she was normal.' The key word here means 'I', which Senya combines with 'thought'. Then Fedya replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты уголь пил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you drink charcoal?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'was drinking' is enough to make it a question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ага, две пачки сожрал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yeah, I ate two packs.' Senya puts 'yeah' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'two'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ни хрена себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No shit.' In Russian, 'horseradish' leads into 'yourself' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And Senya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня болит живот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My stomach hurts.' The first content word means 'me', and 'belly' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе надо пойти домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You need to go home.' Listen for the word meaning 'for you' right alongside 'it is necessary' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу работать сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I cannot work today.' Listen for the word meaning 'I' right alongside 'can' in this line. Fedya answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Иди домой и отдыхай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go home and rest.' A compact expression built around 'go' and 'rest'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
